  {
    "episode_id": 78159,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78159.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 128,
    "turn_text": "The way I look at this coming Congress is the worst won't happen as a result of the House flipping. The Democrats will no longer be able to pass through a process called reconciliation where they can do it with only their votes. Won't be able to do things like they did in this Congress that just ended The $1.9 trillion so-called American rescue package, which created 40-year inflation. That's not me saying that. That's Larry Summers, Bill Clinton, the Secretary of the Treasurer. And then they doubled down by adding another $750 billion last summer. And that's the reason we have this rampant inflation. The way they were able to do that, Terry, without a single Republican vote is there is one way around the filibuster in the Senate. call reconciliation and they use that process and were able to pass it with not a single Republican vote in the House or the Senate. Since the House is non-Republican hands, the worst will not happen. On the proactive side, we'll see what we can agree with. Remember, the President has to sign something for it to become law, and it has to get through the Senate, and it takes 60 votes to get most things through the Senate. We'll see what we can agree on, but I hope we'll look for some things that are worth doing that aren't overly partisan using a football analogy, things between the 40 art lines. that both sides can agree on and make some progress for the country. But the worst won't happen. Thank goodness the house flipped. And I think that prevents the worst.",
